e  yard like practicing like uh with their  rifles and stuff like that on like  little zombie targets and [ __ ] like that  like that's their class that they call  it like outdoor activities or something  like that so the funniest thing about  all this is that it's all like couched  in this idyllic atmosphere of a very  wholesome like 1950s sitcom like Lassie  or Leave it to Beaver not like in a real  self-aware kind of way you know what I  mean like because you know how there's  that kind of different difference where  it's like something could just either be  genuinely like that or it's like that  and it's like aware that it's like that  so it's kind of like winking at the  audience this one is not really winking  at the audience which I think makes it  funnier  so I mean none of the characters in the  movie like think any of this stuff  that's going on is particularly weird or  Sinister like why would they um that's  just like the way that they grew up this  is the world that they know it's just  the way that things are and this is the  way that things have to be like in order  to maintain their kind of like cushy  lifestyles in Suburbia right so nobody  um really even seems all that bothered  by the fact that anybody that causes any  kind of dissension is apparently sent to  what they call the wild Zone which is  like outside of the safety of the town  where all the zombies like are allowed  to kind of roam around uncontained and  you sort of have to like fend for  yourself you don't have zomcon out there  to like protect you so the action in the  movie takes place in this town called  Willard and we're following the Robinson  family so the mom is named Helen and  she's played by Carrie Anne Moss who is  great in this uh dad is called Bill and  he's played by Dylan Baker who is also  great and their son Timmy who's probably  like 11 11 12 years old something like  that who's played by cassan Ray now  initially the Robinsons are kind of  portrayed as this very stereotypical  like 50s family ideal mom is like this  dutiful housewife who always looks  perfect like with the pearls and the  high heels never has a hair out of place  all that kind of stuff and dad is kind  of like sort of goofy distant uninvolved  like he just wants to while away his  weekends playing golf with the boys he  doesn't really want to spend time  hanging out with his wife or his kids or  anything like that so there's also kind  of like this unspoken I guess like  undercurrent of sort of consumerism and  like keeping up with the Joneses and  stuff like that very much like something  from the 50s would have been uh  appearances are Paramount like it's the  most important thing so when the new  head of security at zomcon who is a guy  named Jonathan bottoms and he uh moves  in or he's played by um Henry zerny I  guess is how you pronounce that guy's  last name uh he moves in with his family  like across the street from the the  Robinsons and at that point like Helen  starts to agitate for their family like  getting a zombie Serpent of their very  own because she's really really  embarrassed to be in the only family in  town that doesn't have at least one  zombie because she's like saying to her  husband she's like you know they you  know the new head of security had just  moved in across the street they have six  zombies and we don't even have one and  like people are talking you know what I  mean so it was that kind of stuff now  the reason that they don't have a zombie  is because of Bill now although bill  will never admit this out loud bill is  actually kind of like terrified of  zombies ever since he was forced to kill  his dad when he was a child like after  his dad zombified in front of him and  like tried to eat him so he kind of has  a lot of like residual trauma about that  but Helen of course like I said she's  very um status conscious so she kind of  goes behind his back and buys a zombie  for them anyway and although like Bill  is pretty pis